2-2019","pixels":312,"insar_mm_yr":-5.65},{"id":"SCFJ","gps_mm_yr":-1.93,"gps_sigma":0.87,"used":true,"years":"2018-2025","pixels":203,"insar_mm_yr":-1.77},{"id":"SCHA","gps_mm_yr":-1.54,"gps_sigma":0.74,"used":true,"years":"2009-2018","pixels":237,"insar_mm_yr":-1.7}],"gps_summary":"One constant ties the radar to the 4 GPS stations inside the map that have a well-determined rate. After that, radar and GPS vertical rates differ by 0.4 mm a year (root mean square). With so few stations that figure flatters the radar, so every rate's uncertainty uses the GPS stations' own uncertainty instead, 0.8 mm a year. Counting all 5 stations, including those outside the map or with a very uncertain GPS rate (marked *), the difference is 0.8 mm a year. Some GPS records ended before the radar record began in 2016, so part of the difference is the two instruments measuring different years."},"nisar":{"ok":true,"summary":"NISAR's L-band radar passed over Charleston 10 times between June 2026 and October 2026. Adding its 9 consecutive interferograms gives the ground motion over those 120 days. Over that time the long-term rates predict that neighborhoods should differ from each other by only about 0.8 mm. NISAR's neighborhood values scatter by about 14 mm. That scatter is the atmosphere and ionosphere delaying the radar signal, not the ground moving, so NISAR cannot yet confirm or contradict the long-term rates. It needs a longer record before the noise averages down to millimeters. What NISAR shows already is reach. In a typical 12-day pair it returned a measurement over 91% of the land in these neighborhoods (between 65% and 99%), where the Sentinel-1 record gives a reliable long-term rate over 62%. Each figure uses its own product's quality flags, so this is not a like-for-like test, but it points to NISAR filling in marsh edges and wooded ground that are blank today.","product":"NISAR L2 GUNW (provisional)","doi":"10.5067/NIL2GUNW-P1","track_frame":"098_D_072","band":"L","polariz